1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,260
It's 11:47 at night.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,280
A mother and her daughter are standing by a window.

3
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:08,680
Outside, the moon is shining above the ocean.

4
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,960
Then, without warning, it disappears.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,420
The little girl points at the sky.

6
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:16,560
Mom, where did it go?

7
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,700
Her mother has no answer.

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,760
Within minutes, videos begin spreading across the internet.

9
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,740
People gather in the streets.

10
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:27,040
News channels interrupt their broadcasts.

11
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:31,260
Astronomers turn their telescopes toward an empty patch of sky.

12
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,380
The moon hasn't exploded.

13
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,680
It hasn't moved behind another planet.

14
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:37,540
It's simply gone.

15
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:40,220
And for the first time in human history,

16
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,280
Earth has no moon.

17
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:45,000
But here's what nobody realizes yet.

18
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:48,840
The disappearance itself isn't necessarily what would cause the greatest

19
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,860
damage. The consequences could begin unfolding in the oceans,

20
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:57,080
among animals that depend on the moon to survive and reproduce.

21
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,640
This is WHAT IF?

22
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:01,500
The Unknown File

23
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,780
The first morning arrives.

24
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,720
The sun rises as usual.

25
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,440
Electricity still works.

26
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,300
People continue going to work.

27
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:13,400
And despite the extraordinary event, the world hasn't ended.

28
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:17,960
But scientists around the globe are already monitoring something unusual.

29
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:19,240
The oceans.

30
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:24,100
For billions of years, the moon has exerted gravitational forces that

31
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,740
help create the tides.

32
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,900
Twice a day in many places, the sea rises and falls.

33
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:33,880
Entire ecosystems have adapted to those repeating cycles.

34
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,240
But now, the moon's contribution is gone.

35
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,680
The sun still produces tides.

36
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,600
The ocean doesn't stop moving.

37
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,760
Yet the familiar tidal patterns begin changing.

38
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:47,080
And somewhere along the California coast,

39
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,700
that change could create a remarkable problem.

40
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:55,320
Beneath a sandy beach, thousands of tiny fish eggs are waiting.

41
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:58,740
They belong to a species called the California grunion.

42
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:02,740
And these fish have one of the strangest reproductive behaviors on

43
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:03,460
Earth.

44
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:08,060
During certain high tides, adult grunion actually swim out of the

45
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:09,820
ocean and onto the beach.

46
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,900
The females bury their eggs in the wet sand.

47
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,820
Then, the fish return to the sea.

48
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:17,560
The eggs remain hidden underground.

49
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:21,840
And roughly 10 days later, another series of high tides normally reaches

50
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,580
them. The waves disturb the eggs.

51
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:25,980
The young fish hatch.

52
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,580
And a new generation enters the ocean.

53
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,520
But imagine those eggs waiting in a world without the moon.

54
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:36,900
The powerful lunar contribution to the tides no longer exists.

55
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:40,820
On some beaches, the remaining tides might not reach the places where

56
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,160
eggs have been buried.

57
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:44,720
The young fish could remain trapped.

58
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:47,100
Days pass. Then weeks.

59
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,420
Some eggs might eventually hatch if waves reach them.

60
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:53,520
Others could remain buried too long and die.

61
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,920
And if those changes repeatedly interfered with spawning,

62
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:59,780
entire local populations could struggle.

63
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:03,120
This isn't because the moon directly keeps the fish alive.

64
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:06,920
It's because their reproduction has evolved around a rhythm the moon

65
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,100
helps create.

66
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:12,600
Remove that rhythm, and the system that once worked so precisely may

67
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:13,460
begin failing.

68
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:17,340
But the consequences wouldn't stop at a California beach.

69
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:21,180
Far away, beneath the warm waters of tropical oceans,

70
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:24,820
scientists could encounter an even more extraordinary problem.

71
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:26,200
Coral reefs.

72
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:29,540
They're among the most diverse ecosystems on Earth.

73
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:32,980
And although they may look like underwater plants or rocks,

74
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:35,080
corals are living animals.

75
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:39,300
Some reproduce through remarkable synchronized spawning events.

76
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:43,360
On certain nights, thousands of coral colonies release reproductive

77
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:44,880
cells into the ocean.

78
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:49,140
Sometimes the water becomes filled with tiny floating bundles.

79
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:52,740
And those bundles must encounter one another for fertilization to

80
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:53,660
take place.

81
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:55,440
Timing is critical.

82
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,000
If the cells are released too far apart,

83
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:01,020
the chances of successful reproduction can decrease.

84
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:04,600
So how do corals coordinate these enormous events?

85
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:08,580
For some species, changes in moonlight provide important signals.

86
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:13,080
The gradual progression of the lunar cycle helps synchronize spawning.

87
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:16,900
But now imagine a marine biologist watching a reef in our moonless

88
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:20,140
world. The expected spawning season arrives.

89
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:23,200
The usual lunar light signals never do.

90
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:26,100
Some corals might still respond to temperature,

91
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,900
seasonal changes, or other environmental cues.

92
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:32,480
Others could struggle to synchronize their reproductive timing.

93
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,980
Instead of releasing their cells together,

94
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:37,780
spawning could become scattered across different nights.

95
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,900
And in some places, that could mean fewer successfully fertilized

96
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:46,560
eggs, fewer larvae, and fewer new coral colonies growing into the

97
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:47,760
next generation.

98
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:50,520
The reef wouldn't necessarily die overnight,

99
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:53,540
but imagine this disruption happening repeatedly,

100
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:55,320
year after year.

101
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:58,280
Coral populations already facing pollution,

102
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:02,220
warming oceans, and other pressures could become less capable of recovering

103
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:03,320
from damage.

104
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:07,100
And when coral reefs struggle, the consequences can spread through

105
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:08,240
the ecosystem.

106
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:11,740
Because reefs aren't simply beautiful underwater landscapes,

107
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:16,260
they provide shelter, feeding grounds, and nursery habitats for extraordinary

108
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:18,900
numbers of fish and other marine creatures.

109
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:23,560
A damaged reef can mean fewer suitable places for young fish to survive.

110
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:27,740
Over time, that could affect the abundance of certain fish populations.

111
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:31,580
And that's where the consequences could begin affecting human lives.

112
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:35,960
Imagine a fishing community that has depended on a nearby reef for

113
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:36,900
generations.

114
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:40,320
A father takes his boat out as he has done for years.

115
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:41,840
He knows the currents.

116
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:43,440
He knows the fishing grounds.

117
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,800
He knows where fish used to gather.

118
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,760
But in one possible future, the ecosystem has changed.

119
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:53,060
Some familiar fishing grounds are no longer as productive.

120
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:54,760
A catch is smaller.

121
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:59,340
A family business that once supported several generations faces increasing

122
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:00,000
uncertainty.

123
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:04,460
And this is only one example of how environmental changes might eventually

124
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:05,580
reach people.

125
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:09,260
Of course, not every reef would respond in the same way.

126
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,860
Not every fish population would collapse.

127
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,520
Nature is extraordinarily complicated, and some species might adapt.

128
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:20,060
But the disappearance of the moon would remove environmental signals

129
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:22,940
that many living things have evolved to use.

130
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:26,980
And the consequences would not necessarily be distributed equally.

131
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:28,980
Now, let's return to land.

132
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:33,120
Every night, the sky remains darker without moonlight.

133
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:36,460
In remote areas, the change is especially noticeable.

134
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:40,300
For many animals, moonlight is much more than something illuminating

135
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:41,440
the landscape.

136
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,240
It influences when they hunt, where they travel,

137
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,860
and how easily they detect danger.

138
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:50,360
Consider a small nocturnal animal searching for food.

139
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:54,380
On a bright moonlit night, it may be easier for a predator to see

140
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:58,560
it. But that same light may also help the prey detect an approaching

141
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:02,760
threat. The balance depends on the species and the environment.

142
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,380
Without the changing cycle of moonlight,

143
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:08,540
familiar patterns of nighttime activity could shift.

144
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:11,140
Some predators might find new opportunities.

145
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:13,740
Some prey might become more vulnerable.

146
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,940
Others could benefit from the permanent absence of bright lunar nights.

147
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:22,660
Over time, those behavioral changes could influence survival and population

148
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:27,240
numbers. But the changes to Earth wouldn't only involve living creatures.

149
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:30,960
There is another role the moon has played over billions of years.

150
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:35,080
It has helped influence the stability of Earth's rotational axis.

151
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:39,400
Our planet is tilted, and that tilt is one of the main reasons we

152
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:40,900
experience seasons.

153
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,600
Without the moon's gravitational influence,

154
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:47,120
Earth's rotational behavior could evolve differently over very long

155
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:51,440
periods. Scientists have studied hypothetical moonless Earths using

156
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:52,980
computer simulations.

157
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:57,380
Some models suggest much greater variations in the tilt of Earth's

158
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,000
axis over geological time.

159
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:01,840
Other models find more constrained changes.

160
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:05,520
So, we cannot honestly predict that Earth's climate would suddenly

161
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:09,580
collapse. But we can say that removing the moon would change one of

162
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:13,380
the major influences on our planet's long-term orientation.

163
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:16,340
And over hundreds of thousands or millions of years,

164
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:20,160
that could affect how sunlight is distributed across Earth,

165
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:24,040
potentially changing the character of seasons and influencing climate

166
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:24,900
patterns.

167
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:28,700
Now imagine returning to our little girl from the opening.

168
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:30,300
Decades have passed.

169
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,940
She's an adult now, and she's standing beside the same window where

170
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:35,860
she watched the moon disappear.

171
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,320
She remembers the night the entire world looked up,

172
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:44,200
the panic, the confusion, the extraordinary moment humanity realized

173
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:45,540
something was missing.

174
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:48,480
But the world around her is still alive.

175
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:50,040
People have built cities.

176
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:51,860
Families have raised children.

177
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:54,160
Scientists have adapted their models.

178
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:55,880
And life has continued.

179
00:08:56,260 --> 00:09:00,180
Yet, our planet no longer follows every rhythm it once did.

180
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:03,460
Certain coastlines experience different tidal patterns.

181
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:05,560
Some ecosystems have changed.

182
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:08,660
And the night sky is permanently transformed.

183
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:10,420
She looks outside.

184
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:11,800
There are stars.

185
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:13,460
There are distant planets.

186
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:14,380
But no moon.

187
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:19,040
And somewhere beneath the ocean, the struggle to adapt continues.

188
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:23,180
The most unsettling part of this thought experiment isn't that Earth

189
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:25,000
might suddenly be destroyed.

190
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:27,740
It's that something could disappear from the sky,

191
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:31,840
and living things all around the world might spend generations dealing

192
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:33,200
with the consequences.

193
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:34,780
Some could adapt.

194
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:36,240
Some could struggle.

195
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:39,280
And some future lives might never begin.

196
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,540
So the next time you look at the moon, remember,

197
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:45,580
you're not simply looking at a light in the night sky.

198
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:48,720
You're looking at something that has helped shape the rhythms of life

199
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:51,340
on Earth for billions of years.

200
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:55,360
What if… The Unknown File

